/* Edit the question/answer pairs here, then run python3 build.py. */
const CARD_CONTENT = {
  1: [
    ["What are the seven characteristics of living organisms?", "MRS GREN: movement, respiration, sensitivity, growth, reproduction, excretion and nutrition."],
    ["How do respiration, excretion and growth differ?", "Respiration releases energy from nutrients in cells. Excretion removes metabolic waste and excess substances. Growth is a permanent increase in size and dry mass."],
    ["What is a species, and how is its scientific name written?", "A species can reproduce together to produce fertile offspring. A binomial name gives genus then species: Homo sapiens. Capitalise the genus, use lower case for the species, and italicise both when typed."],
    ["What evidence helps classify organisms by ancestry?", "Shared anatomy and morphology, plus similarities in corresponding DNA and amino acid sequences. Greater sequence similarity generally suggests a more recent common ancestor."],
    ["What distinguishes the five kingdoms?", "Animals feed on organic material; plants have cellulose walls and photosynthetic cells; fungi have chitin walls and absorb externally digested food; protoctists are varied eukaryotes; prokaryotes lack a membrane-bound nucleus."],
    ["Why are viruses different from bacteria?", "Bacteria are cells with cytoplasm, ribosomes and circular DNA. Viruses are genetic material inside a protein coat, reproduce only inside host cells and are outside the five cellular kingdoms."],
    ["Which features identify the five vertebrate groups?", "Fish: gills and fins. Amphibians: moist skin. Reptiles: dry, scaly skin. Birds: feathers. Mammals: hair and milk-producing mammary glands."],
    ["How can you identify the four arthropod groups?", "All have jointed legs, segmented bodies and an exoskeleton. Insects: 6 legs, 1 antenna pair. Arachnids: 8 legs, no antennae. Crustaceans: 2 antenna pairs. Myriapods: many leg-bearing segments."],
    ["How do ferns, flowering plants, monocots and dicots differ?", "Ferns reproduce by spores; flowering plants make seeds enclosed in fruits. Monocots have one cotyledon and usually parallel leaf veins. Dicots have two cotyledons and usually branching veins."],
    ["How do you use a dichotomous key?", "Start at the first pair of contrasting statements. Choose the one matching the specimen, then follow its instruction. Use observable features rather than subjective descriptions such as 'large'."]
  ],
  2: [
    ["What is the order of biological organisation?", "Cell → tissue → organ → organ system → organism. A tissue is similar cells working together; an organ contains different tissues; an organ system contains cooperating organs."],
    ["What do the membrane, cytoplasm and nucleus do?", "The cell membrane controls entry and exit. Cytoplasm is the site of many chemical reactions. The nucleus contains genetic information controlling cell activities."],
    ["What do mitochondria, ribosomes, rough ER and vesicles do?", "Mitochondria carry out aerobic respiration. Ribosomes make proteins. Rough ER processes and transports proteins made by its attached ribosomes. Vesicles transport substances in membrane-bound sacs."],
    ["Which extra structures occur in typical plant cells?", "A cellulose wall supports the cell; a permanent cell-sap vacuole maintains turgor; chloroplasts absorb light for photosynthesis. Chloroplasts occur in photosynthetic cells, so root hair cells normally lack them."],
    ["How is a bacterial cell organised?", "It has a wall, membrane, cytoplasm, ribosomes and circular DNA free in the cytoplasm. Plasmids are smaller DNA rings. It lacks a membrane-bound nucleus, mitochondria and rough ER."],
    ["How are root hair, palisade and xylem cells adapted?", "Root hairs provide a large absorbing area. Palisade cells have many chloroplasts for photosynthesis. Xylem forms dead, hollow tubes with strong lignified walls for water transport and support."],
    ["How do red blood cells, phagocytes and lymphocytes differ?", "Red cells contain haemoglobin and have a biconcave shape and no nucleus, aiding oxygen transport. Phagocytes engulf pathogens. Lymphocytes produce specific antibodies."],
    ["How do neurones, muscle cells, ciliated cells and goblet cells work?", "Long neurone fibres carry impulses; myelin speeds transmission. Muscle cells contract and contain many mitochondria. Cilia move mucus; goblet cells secrete it."],
    ["How are sperm and egg cells adapted?", "Sperm have a swimming tail, many mitochondria and a haploid nucleus. Eggs have a haploid nucleus and a large cytoplasm containing nutrient reserves for early development."],
    ["How do you calculate magnification and actual size?", "Magnification = image size ÷ actual size. Actual size = image size ÷ magnification. Use the same units first: 1 mm = 1000 µm. Magnification has no unit."]
  ],
  3: [
    ["What is diffusion?", "The net movement of particles from higher to lower concentration, down a concentration gradient, because particles move randomly. It needs no energy from respiration."],
    ["Which factors increase diffusion rate?", "A steeper concentration gradient, larger surface area, shorter diffusion distance and higher temperature. Higher temperature gives particles more kinetic energy."],
    ["What is osmosis?", "The net movement of water molecules from higher to lower water potential through a partially permeable membrane. Water usually moves from a more dilute to a more concentrated solution."],
    ["What happens to cells in a solution with higher water potential?", "Water enters by osmosis. Plant cells become turgid as the vacuole expands; their walls resist bursting. Animal cells swell and may burst because they lack a cell wall."],
    ["What happens to cells in a solution with lower water potential?", "Water leaves by osmosis. Animal cells shrink. Plant cells become flaccid and may plasmolyse: the membrane pulls away from the cell wall."],
    ["How does active transport differ from diffusion?", "Active transport uses membrane carrier proteins and energy from respiration to move particles against their gradient, from lower to higher concentration. Examples include mineral-ion uptake by roots and glucose absorption in the intestine."],
    ["At equilibrium, have particles stopped moving?", "No. Particles still move randomly in both directions, but there is no net movement. Equal water potential means no net osmosis."],
    ["How do you investigate osmosis in potato cylinders?", "Use equal-sized cylinders, record initial mass, immerse in different sugar concentrations for equal times, then blot and reweigh. Keep temperature and volumes constant; repeat. Percentage change = (final − initial) ÷ initial × 100. Zero change estimates equal water potential."]
  ],
  4: [
    ["What are carbohydrates, proteins and fats built from?", "Starch, glycogen and cellulose contain glucose units. Proteins contain amino acids. A triglyceride contains glycerol and three fatty acids."],
    ["Which elements occur in carbohydrates, fats and proteins?", "Carbohydrates and fats contain carbon, hydrogen and oxygen. Proteins also contain nitrogen; some contain sulphur."],
    ["What are the roles of starch, glycogen, cellulose and fats?", "Starch stores carbohydrate in plants; glycogen stores it in animals. Cellulose supports plant cell walls. Fats provide long-term energy storage and insulation."],
    ["Why does a protein's amino acid sequence matter?", "The sequence determines folding, shape and function. High temperature or extreme pH can denature the protein, changing binding sites and preventing normal function."],
    ["How is DNA structured?", "Two strands form a double helix. Each nucleotide contains a sugar, phosphate and base. Complementary bases pair: A with T, and C with G. A gene is a length of DNA coding for a protein."],
    ["Why is water useful in organisms?", "It is a solvent and transport medium. Dissolved substances react in cytoplasm, travel in plasma and can be removed in urine."],
    ["How do you test for starch and reducing sugar?", "Starch: add iodine; a positive result is blue-black. Reducing sugar: add Benedict's solution and heat in a hot water bath; a positive result is a green to brick-red precipitate."],
    ["How do you test for protein and lipid?", "Protein: add Biuret reagent; blue turns violet/purple. Lipid: shake with ethanol, then add the extract to water; a white milky emulsion forms. Keep flammable ethanol away from flames."],
    ["How can DCPIP compare vitamin C concentration?", "Add each sample to the same volume and concentration of blue DCPIP until it becomes colourless. Less sample needed means a higher vitamin C concentration. Keep the endpoint consistent and repeat."],
    ["What makes a food-test comparison fair?", "Use equal sample and reagent volumes and the same heating time and temperature. Use a distilled-water negative control and a known positive sample. A negative result means 'not detected', rather than proving complete absence."]
  ],
  5: [
    ["What is an enzyme?", "A protein acting as a biological catalyst. It increases reaction rate, lowers activation energy and remains chemically unchanged at the end."],
    ["Why are enzymes specific?", "Their active sites have shapes complementary to particular substrates. Binding forms an enzyme–substrate complex; products leave and the enzyme can act again."],
    ["Why does warming initially increase enzyme activity?", "Particles gain kinetic energy, causing more frequent successful enzyme–substrate collisions, so reaction rate rises towards the optimum."],
    ["Why does enzyme activity fall above the optimum temperature?", "The enzyme denatures: its structure and active-site shape change, so substrates can no longer bind normally. Low temperatures usually slow enzymes without denaturing them."],
    ["How does pH affect enzyme activity?", "Each enzyme has an optimum pH. Moving away from it can alter active-site shape and reduce binding; extreme pH can denature the enzyme. Pepsin works in acidic stomach conditions."],
    ["Why does a substrate-concentration graph reach a plateau?", "All available enzyme active sites become occupied, so enzyme availability limits the rate. Adding more substrate alone cannot increase it further."],
    ["How do intracellular and extracellular enzymes differ?", "Both are made in cells. Intracellular enzymes act inside cells, such as respiration enzymes. Extracellular enzymes are secreted and act outside their cells, such as digestive enzymes in the gut."],
    ["How can you measure an enzyme reaction rate?", "For catalase, measure oxygen volume ÷ time. For amylase, sample into iodine until starch is no longer detected; relative rate = 1 ÷ endpoint time. Control pH, temperature and starting quantities except the variable being investigated; repeat."]
  ],
  6: [
    ["What is photosynthesis, and what is its balanced equation?", "Plants make carbohydrates from carbon dioxide and water using light energy absorbed by chlorophyll. 6CO₂ + 6H₂O → C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂. Light is an energy source, not a chemical raw material."],
    ["What can a plant do with glucose?", "Respire it; store it as starch; make cellulose, lipids or sucrose; or combine its products with nitrogen compounds to make amino acids and proteins. Sucrose is transported in phloem."],
    ["How do leaves maximise light absorption?", "A broad lamina intercepts light. The transparent upper epidermis lets light reach palisade cells, which contain many chloroplasts near the upper surface."],
    ["How do leaves support exchange and transport?", "A thin leaf gives a short diffusion distance; spongy mesophyll air spaces and stomata allow gas movement. Xylem supplies water and minerals; phloem carries sucrose and amino acids. The cuticle limits water loss."],
    ["Why do plants need nitrate and magnesium ions?", "Nitrates provide nitrogen for amino acids and proteins; shortage reduces growth. Magnesium is needed for chlorophyll; shortage causes yellowing and reduces photosynthesis."],
    ["What is a limiting factor in photosynthesis?", "The factor in shortest supply relative to demand. Light, carbon dioxide or temperature may limit the rate. A plateau means another factor has become limiting, rather than photosynthesis stopping."],
    ["Do plants respire only in darkness?", "No. Plants respire day and night. In bright light, photosynthesis can exceed respiration, producing net oxygen release. In darkness, photosynthesis stops while respiration continues."],
    ["How do you test a leaf for starch?", "Boil in water, heat in ethanol in a hot water bath to remove chlorophyll, rinse in warm water, then add iodine. Blue-black indicates starch. Never heat flammable ethanol directly over a flame."],
    ["Why destarch a plant before testing photosynthesis requirements?", "Darkness reduces stored starch, so starch detected later can be linked to the experimental conditions. Compare exposed and covered leaf areas for light, or green and non-green areas for chlorophyll."],
    ["How can you measure photosynthesis rate in an aquatic plant?", "Collect oxygen volume over a fixed time; rate = volume ÷ time. Change one factor and control plant size, temperature, carbon dioxide supply and time. Allow acclimatisation and repeat. Gas volume is more reliable than bubble counts because bubble sizes vary."]
  ],
  7: [
    ["What makes a balanced diet?", "Appropriate quantities and proportions of carbohydrates, fats, proteins, vitamins, minerals, water and fibre. Needs vary with age, body size, activity and growth; both too little and too much can cause malnutrition."],
    ["Which nutrient deficiencies should you recognise?", "Vitamin C deficiency causes scurvy. Vitamin D deficiency can cause rickets. Iron deficiency causes anaemia through reduced haemoglobin. Calcium supports bones and teeth; fibre helps prevent constipation."],
    ["How do mechanical and chemical digestion differ?", "Mechanical digestion breaks food into smaller pieces without changing molecules, increasing surface area. Chemical digestion uses enzymes to break large insoluble molecules into small soluble molecules."],
    ["What is the route through the alimentary canal?", "Mouth → oesophagus → stomach → duodenum and the rest of the small intestine → colon → rectum → anus. Peristalsis moves food using waves of muscle contraction and relaxation."],
    ["What are the main digestive enzymes and their products?", "Amylase: starch → maltose. Maltase: maltose → glucose. Proteases and peptidases: proteins → amino acids overall. Lipase: fats → fatty acids and glycerol."],
    ["What do stomach acid and bile do?", "Stomach hydrochloric acid kills many bacteria and provides acidic conditions for pepsin. Bile is made by the liver, stored in the gall bladder and released into the duodenum; it helps neutralise acid and emulsifies fats. Bile is not an enzyme."],
    ["How is a villus adapted for absorption?", "Villi and microvilli provide a large surface area. A one-cell-thick epithelium gives a short diffusion distance. Capillary blood flow maintains gradients. Lacteals transport most absorbed fat products via lymph."],
    ["How do absorption, assimilation and egestion differ?", "Absorption moves digested nutrients into blood or lymph. Assimilation is their uptake and use by cells. Egestion removes undigested and unabsorbed food as faeces; it differs from excretion of metabolic waste."],
    ["How does tooth decay develop?", "Plaque bacteria use sugars and make acids that dissolve enamel and dentine. Damage reaching the pulp causes pain. Brushing, fluoride and less frequent sugary foods help reduce decay."],
    ["How does cholera cause dehydration?", "Bacterial toxin causes chloride ions to enter the gut lumen, lowering its water potential. Water follows by osmosis, causing diarrhoea and salt loss. Oral rehydration solution supplies water, salts and glucose to replace losses."]
  ],
  8: [
    ["What do xylem and phloem transport?", "Xylem carries water and mineral ions mainly from roots towards leaves. Phloem carries sucrose and amino acids from sources to sinks, upwards or downwards in different tubes."],
    ["How are xylem vessels adapted?", "Dead cells join into hollow continuous tubes without cross walls, reducing resistance to flow. Thick lignified walls withstand tension, prevent collapse and support the plant."],
    ["How do root hair cells absorb water and mineral ions?", "Water enters by osmosis from higher soil water potential to lower cell water potential. Mineral ions can enter by active transport using energy from respiration. The long hair increases absorbing surface area."],
    ["What is transpiration?", "Loss of water vapour from leaves: water evaporates from moist mesophyll cell surfaces into air spaces, then water vapour diffuses through stomata down its concentration gradient."],
    ["How does transpiration pull move water up xylem?", "Evaporation and diffusion remove water from leaves. Replacement water is pulled from xylem, creating tension. Cohesion keeps the water column continuous, transmitting the pull down towards the roots."],
    ["Why does hot, dry, windy weather usually increase transpiration?", "Higher temperature increases evaporation. Low humidity steepens the water-vapour gradient. Wind removes humid air around leaves, maintaining that gradient. Severe water shortage can close stomata and limit loss."],
    ["How do light and water shortage affect stomata and wilting?", "Light commonly opens stomata, increasing vapour loss. If water loss exceeds uptake, cells lose turgor and wilt. Closing stomata limits further loss but also restricts carbon dioxide entry and photosynthesis."],
    ["What are sources and sinks in translocation?", "A source supplies sucrose or amino acids; a sink uses or stores them. A potato tuber is a sink while storing starch and becomes a source when reserves support sprouting."],
    ["What does a potometer measure?", "Water uptake, which estimates transpiration rather than measuring it exactly. Rate = capillary cross-sectional area × bubble distance ÷ time. Some absorbed water is used or retained by the plant."],
    ["How do you set up a reliable potometer investigation?", "Cut the shoot under water and use a water-filled, airtight apparatus. Change one condition, controlling leaf area and other environmental factors. Allow adjustment, time bubble movement, repeat and calculate means."]
  ],
  9: [
    ["Why is mammalian circulation called double circulation?", "Blood passes through the heart twice per complete circuit: heart → lungs → heart → body. This restores pressure after the lungs while keeping pulmonary pressure relatively low."],
    ["What is the full blood pathway through the heart?", "Body → vena cava → right atrium → right ventricle → pulmonary artery → lungs → pulmonary veins → left atrium → left ventricle → aorta → body. Valves prevent backflow; the septum separates the sides."],
    ["Why is the left ventricle wall thickest?", "It generates higher pressure to drive blood around the systemic circuit. The right ventricle pumps to the lungs at lower pressure. Both normally pump the same volume per beat."],
    ["How do arteries and veins differ?", "Arteries carry blood away from the heart and have thick muscular, elastic walls for high pressure. Veins return blood at low pressure, with wider lumens and valves in many veins. Direction defines them, rather than oxygen content."],
    ["How are capillaries adapted for exchange?", "Their walls are one cell thick, giving a short diffusion distance. Narrow lumens and extensive networks bring blood close to tissues across a large total surface area."],
    ["What does plasma transport, and how do red cells carry oxygen?", "Plasma carries dissolved nutrients, ions, hormones, urea and much carbon dioxide, and distributes heat. Red cells contain haemoglobin, which reversibly binds oxygen; their biconcave shape and lack of nucleus aid transport."],
    ["What do phagocytes, lymphocytes and platelets do?", "Phagocytes engulf and digest pathogens. Lymphocytes make specific antibodies. Platelets help initiate clotting, converting soluble fibrinogen to insoluble fibrin fibres that trap blood cells."],
    ["Why does heart rate rise during exercise?", "Active muscles respire faster. Faster circulation delivers more oxygen and glucose and removes more carbon dioxide, supporting the higher energy demand."],
    ["How does coronary heart disease affect the heart?", "Fatty deposits narrow coronary arteries and reduce oxygen delivery to heart muscle. A clot may block flow and cause a heart attack. Smoking, inactivity and high saturated-fat intake are modifiable risk factors."],
    ["What does the hepatic portal vein do?", "It carries absorbed nutrients from the intestine to the liver. The hepatic artery supplies the liver with oxygenated blood; the hepatic vein carries blood away from it."]
  ],
  10: [
    ["What is a pathogen, and how can it spread?", "A disease-causing organism or agent. Transmission can occur through contact, body fluids, air droplets, contaminated food or water, surfaces or vectors."],
    ["How do physical and chemical barriers prevent infection?", "Skin blocks entry. Nose hairs and mucus trap particles and pathogens. Cilia move mucus towards the throat. Stomach hydrochloric acid kills many swallowed pathogens."],
    ["How does phagocytosis work?", "A phagocyte surrounds and engulfs a pathogen into a vesicle, then digests it with enzymes. It is different from antibody production by lymphocytes."],
    ["How are antigens and antibodies related?", "Antigens are recognised as foreign. Lymphocytes produce antibodies with complementary binding sites, so each antibody is specific to particular antigens. Antibodies can neutralise pathogens or mark them for destruction."],
    ["How does vaccination produce protection?", "Harmless forms of antigens stimulate specific lymphocytes to multiply, produce antibodies and form memory cells. Later exposure to the same antigen triggers a faster, larger response."],
    ["How do active and passive immunity differ?", "Active immunity uses your own antibody production and produces memory cells, usually giving longer protection. Passive immunity transfers ready-made antibodies, acts quickly and is temporary because it produces no memory cells through the transfer."],
    ["How do hygiene and sanitation reduce transmission?", "Handwashing reduces transfer to the mouth. Cooking kills many pathogens. Sewage treatment and safe drinking water interrupt faecal contamination. Each measure blocks a particular route, rather than simply 'improving hygiene'."],
    ["What makes type 1 diabetes an autoimmune disease?", "The immune system destroys the body's own insulin-producing pancreatic cells. Insulin secretion falls and blood-glucose control is impaired. It is not a transmissible infection."]
  ],
  11: [
    ["How do ventilation, gas exchange and respiration differ?", "Ventilation moves air into and out of lungs. Gas exchange transfers oxygen and carbon dioxide across an exchange surface. Respiration is the cellular chemical process releasing energy from nutrients."],
    ["What route does air take to the exchange surface?", "Nose or mouth → larynx → trachea → bronchi → bronchioles → alveoli. Cartilage keeps the trachea open. Goblet cells make mucus; cilia move it towards the throat."],
    ["How are alveoli adapted for gas exchange?", "Many alveoli give a large surface area. Alveolar and capillary walls are each one cell thick. A moist lining helps gases dissolve. Blood flow and ventilation maintain concentration gradients."],
    ["Which way do oxygen and carbon dioxide diffuse?", "Oxygen diffuses from alveolar air into blood, where it binds haemoglobin. Carbon dioxide diffuses from arriving blood into alveoli. Each gas moves down its own concentration gradient."],
    ["What causes inspiration?", "The diaphragm contracts and flattens; external intercostal muscles raise ribs up and out. Thoracic volume increases, lung pressure falls below atmospheric pressure, and air enters."],
    ["What causes quiet expiration?", "The diaphragm and external intercostal muscles relax. The diaphragm domes, ribs move down and in, and elastic recoil reduces lung volume. Pressure rises above atmospheric pressure and air leaves."],
    ["How does expired air differ from inspired air?", "It usually contains less oxygen, more carbon dioxide and water vapour, and is warmer. Approximate oxygen values are 21% inspired and 16% expired; carbon dioxide is about 0.04% inspired and 4% expired."],
    ["Why do breathing rate and depth rise during exercise?", "Faster muscle respiration increases carbon dioxide in blood. The brain detects the change and increases stimulation of breathing muscles. Greater ventilation maintains gas-exchange gradients."]
  ],
  12: [
    ["What is respiration, and why do cells need it?", "Chemical reactions in cells release energy from nutrient molecules. Energy supports muscle contraction, active transport, protein synthesis, growth, cell division and other metabolic processes."],
    ["What is the balanced equation for aerobic respiration?", "Glucose + oxygen → carbon dioxide + water. C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂ → 6CO₂ + 6H₂O. It releases a relatively large amount of energy per glucose molecule."],
    ["How does anaerobic respiration in muscle differ?", "It does not use oxygen and releases relatively little energy. Glucose → lactic acid. Carbon dioxide is not a product of this pathway in human muscles."],
    ["What are the products of anaerobic respiration in yeast?", "Ethanol and carbon dioxide, with a small energy release. Glucose → ethanol + carbon dioxide. C₆H₁₂O₆ → 2C₂H₅OH + 2CO₂."],
    ["What is oxygen debt after vigorous exercise?", "The extra oxygen requirement associated with accumulated lactic acid. Blood carries lactic acid to the liver; continued rapid breathing supplies oxygen for its aerobic respiration there."],
    ["Where does respiration happen?", "In living plant and animal cells, day and night. Much aerobic respiration in eukaryotes occurs in mitochondria. Anaerobic reactions occur in the cytoplasm."],
    ["Why does a respirometer use soda lime?", "It absorbs carbon dioxide. As organisms use oxygen, gas volume and pressure fall, so the liquid marker moves towards the organism chamber. The volume decrease can then estimate oxygen uptake."],
    ["How do you calculate and compare respiration rate?", "Oxygen uptake rate = π × radius² × marker distance ÷ time. Use consistent units; radius is half the diameter. Control temperature, check for leaks, include an inert-bead control and divide by organism mass when comparing different masses."]
  ],
  13: [
    ["What is excretion, and how does it differ from egestion?", "Excretion removes metabolic waste, toxic materials and substances in excess. Egestion removes undigested and unabsorbed food as faeces."],
    ["Where are carbon dioxide and urea made and removed?", "Respiration makes carbon dioxide, which is excreted through the lungs. The liver makes urea during processing of excess amino acids; plasma carries it to the kidneys for removal in urine."],
    ["What is deamination?", "Removal of the nitrogen-containing part of excess amino acids in the liver. Toxic ammonia formed during processing is converted to less toxic urea. The remaining carbon-containing part can be respired or stored."],
    ["What route does urine take out of the body?", "Kidney → ureter → bladder → urethra → outside. Ureters carry urine to the bladder; the urethra carries it from the bladder."],
    ["What happens during filtration at the glomerulus?", "High pressure forces water and small dissolved molecules, including glucose, urea and ions, into the renal capsule. Blood cells and large plasma proteins normally remain in the blood."],
    ["What is selectively reabsorbed in the nephron?", "Normally all glucose, needed salts and most water return to blood. Glucose uptake uses active transport; water follows by osmosis. Remaining urea, excess salts and water form urine."],
    ["How are kidney tubule cells adapted for reabsorption?", "Microvilli increase surface area. Many mitochondria supply energy from respiration for active transport of substances such as glucose."],
    ["How do water intake and sweating affect urine?", "High water intake usually produces more dilute urine in a larger volume. Heavy sweating causes water conservation, so urine volume falls and urine becomes more concentrated. Urea production does not stop."]
  ],
  14: [
    ["What links a stimulus to a response?", "A receptor detects a stimulus; coordination sends information to an effector; a muscle or gland produces the response. The CNS is the brain and spinal cord; peripheral nerves connect it to the body."],
    ["What is the sequence of a spinal reflex arc?", "Stimulus → receptor → sensory neurone → relay neurone → motor neurone → effector → response. A reflex is rapid and automatic and does not wait for a conscious decision."],
    ["How does a signal cross a synapse?", "An arriving impulse causes neurotransmitter release from vesicles. It diffuses across the gap and binds complementary receptors, triggering a new impulse. Presynaptic vesicles and postsynaptic receptors ensure one-way transmission."],
    ["What do the cornea, lens, retina and optic nerve do?", "The cornea refracts light. The lens changes shape to focus it. Receptors in the retina detect light. The optic nerve carries impulses to the brain; the blind spot has no light receptors."],
    ["How do rods and cones differ?", "Rods work in dim light and do not distinguish colours. Cones work in brighter light and provide colour vision. The fovea has many cones for detailed vision."],
    ["How does the pupil reflex respond to bright and dim light?", "Bright light: circular iris muscles contract and radial muscles relax, constricting the pupil. Dim light: radial muscles contract and circular muscles relax, dilating it."],
    ["How does the eye focus on near and distant objects?", "Near: ciliary muscles contract, ligaments slacken and the lens becomes thicker, refracting more. Distant: muscles relax, ligaments tighten and the lens becomes thinner, refracting less."],
    ["How does hormonal coordination differ from nervous coordination?", "Hormones are chemical messengers secreted by endocrine glands into blood, acting on target cells with suitable receptors. Hormonal responses are generally slower and longer lasting; nervous responses are generally rapid and brief."],
    ["How does adrenaline prepare the body for action?", "It increases heart rate, breathing rate, pupil diameter and blood glucose, and redirects blood towards muscles. This increases oxygen and glucose delivery for respiration."],
    ["What are homeostasis and negative feedback?", "Homeostasis maintains a relatively constant internal environment. Negative feedback detects a change and produces a response that opposes it; the response reduces as conditions return towards normal."],
    ["How do insulin and glucagon regulate blood glucose?", "High glucose stimulates pancreatic insulin release, promoting glucose uptake and glycogen storage. Low glucose stimulates glucagon release, causing liver glycogen breakdown and glucose release."],
    ["How does the body lose more heat when too hot?", "Sweat evaporates, taking heat from skin. Skin arterioles dilate, increasing blood flow near the surface and heat loss. Hair erector muscles relax. Sweating cools through evaporation, rather than secretion alone."],
    ["How does the body conserve and generate heat when too cold?", "Skin arterioles constrict, reducing surface blood flow. Sweating decreases. Shivering increases muscle activity, respiration and heat release. Raised hairs trap air, although this provides little insulation in humans."],
    ["How does auxin make a shoot bend towards light?", "More auxin accumulates on the shaded side and stimulates greater cell elongation there. Unequal growth bends the shoot towards light: positive phototropism."],
    ["How do roots and shoots respond to gravity?", "Roots are positively gravitropic; shoots are negatively gravitropic. In a horizontal root, auxin builds up below and inhibits elongation there, so the upper side grows faster and the root bends down."]
  ],
  15: [
    ["What is a drug, and how do painkillers and antibiotics differ?", "A drug modifies chemical reactions in the body. Painkillers reduce pain without necessarily removing its cause. Antibiotics kill bacteria or inhibit their growth."],
    ["How can antibiotics affect bacteria?", "They target bacterial processes such as cell-wall formation or protein synthesis. Penicillin interferes with bacterial wall formation, leaving growing cells vulnerable to damage and bursting."],
    ["Why do antibiotics not treat viral infections?", "Viruses lack the bacterial structures and independent metabolic processes targeted by antibiotics. They reproduce using host-cell machinery. A drug targeting bacterial cell walls has no corresponding viral target."],
    ["How does antibiotic resistance become more common?", "Random mutation creates variation. Antibiotic exposure removes susceptible bacteria; resistant bacteria survive, reproduce and pass on resistance. Their proportion rises. The antibiotic selects variants; it does not cause purposeful mutation."],
    ["How can resistance and its spread be reduced?", "Avoid unnecessary antibiotic use to reduce selection pressure. Follow the prescriber's dose and duration instructions. Hygiene and infection control reduce transmission of resistant bacteria. Bacteria become resistant, rather than the patient's body."]
  ],
  16: [
    ["How do asexual and sexual reproduction differ?", "Asexual reproduction uses one parent without gamete fusion and produces genetically identical offspring. Sexual reproduction fuses haploid gamete nuclei into a diploid zygote and produces genetic variation."],
    ["What are the advantages and disadvantages of asexual reproduction?", "It can be fast, needs no mate and preserves useful characteristics. Low genetic variation means offspring may all be vulnerable to the same disease or environmental change."],
    ["What are the main reproductive flower structures?", "The stamen contains an anther producing pollen and a supporting filament. The carpel has a stigma receiving pollen, a style and an ovary containing ovules. Petals and nectar attract animal pollinators."],
    ["How do insect- and wind-pollinated flowers differ?", "Insect flowers commonly have conspicuous petals, nectar, sticky pollen and an internal sticky stigma. Wind flowers have exposed anthers, feathery exposed stigmas and many small, light pollen grains."],
    ["How do pollination, self-pollination and cross-pollination differ?", "Pollination transfers pollen from anther to stigma. Self-pollination stays on the same plant; cross-pollination transfers pollen between different plants of the same species. Self-pollination is still sexual reproduction."],
    ["What happens after compatible pollen reaches a stigma?", "A pollen tube grows through the style to an ovule. A male gamete nucleus travels down it and fuses with the female nucleus. The zygote becomes an embryo, the ovule a seed and usually the ovary a fruit."],
    ["What does a seed need for germination?", "Water, oxygen and a suitable temperature. Water activates processes, oxygen supports respiration and temperature affects enzyme activity. Stored nutrients supply early growth; the radicle usually emerges first."],
    ["What do the main seed structures do?", "The testa protects; cotyledons store or supply nutrients; the radicle forms the young root; the plumule forms the young shoot. Dispersal reduces competition and helps colonise new areas."],
    ["What do testes, sperm ducts and accessory glands do?", "Testes produce sperm and testosterone. The scrotum keeps testes below core body temperature. Sperm ducts carry sperm; accessory glands add fluid to make semen, delivered through the urethra and penis."],
    ["Where do human fertilisation and implantation occur?", "Fertilisation normally occurs in an oviduct. The zygote divides by mitosis into an embryo, which moves to the uterus and implants in its lining. Ovaries produce eggs, oestrogen and progesterone."],
    ["How are human gametes adapted?", "Sperm have a flagellum for swimming, mitochondria for energy and acrosome enzymes to penetrate egg coverings. An egg has nutrient-rich cytoplasm and a coating that changes after fertilisation. Each gamete carries 23 chromosomes; the zygote has 46."],
    ["How do the placenta, umbilical cord and amniotic fluid support a fetus?", "The placenta exchanges oxygen, nutrients and wastes between nearby maternal and fetal blood, which normally do not mix. The cord links fetus and placenta. Amniotic fluid cushions the fetus. Some harmful substances can cross the placenta."],
    ["How do FSH, oestrogen and LH control the menstrual cycle?", "FSH stimulates follicle development and oestrogen secretion. Oestrogen rebuilds the uterine lining and inhibits FSH; high levels trigger an LH surge. LH causes ovulation and formation of the corpus luteum."],
    ["What does progesterone do during the cycle and pregnancy?", "The corpus luteum secretes progesterone to maintain the uterine lining and inhibit FSH and LH. Without pregnancy, hormone levels fall and menstruation follows. During pregnancy, progesterone remains high; the placenta later produces it."],
    ["What is HIV, and how can transmission be reduced?", "HIV is a virus that damages the immune system and can lead to AIDS. It spreads through infected body fluids, blood and mother-to-child routes. Condoms, screened blood and sterile needles reduce transmission."]
  ],
  17: [
    ["How are chromosomes, genes and alleles related?", "Chromosomes are DNA-containing structures carrying genes. A gene is a length of DNA coding for a protein. An allele is an alternative version of a gene."],
    ["How do genotype and phenotype differ?", "Genotype is an organism's allele combination. Phenotype is its observable features, resulting from genotype and environmental influences."],
    ["What do homozygous, heterozygous, dominant and recessive mean?", "Homozygous means two identical alleles; heterozygous means two different alleles. A dominant allele is expressed in a heterozygote; a recessive allele is expressed when no dominant allele of that gene is present."],
    ["What are haploid and diploid cells?", "Haploid cells have one chromosome set; diploid cells have two. Human gametes have 23 chromosomes; typical nucleated body cells have 46 in 23 pairs. Fertilisation restores the diploid number."],
    ["How does DNA determine a protein?", "The DNA base sequence determines the amino acid sequence. An mRNA copy leaves the nucleus and is read by a ribosome, which joins amino acids. The chain folds into a functional protein."],
    ["Why can cells with the same genes have different functions?", "Different genes are expressed in different specialised cells, so they make different proteins. Having a gene does not mean every cell uses it at the same time."],
    ["What does mitosis produce and why is it useful?", "Genetically identical daughter nuclei with chromosome number maintained. It supports growth, repair, replacement and asexual reproduction. A typical diploid cell produces two diploid daughter cells."],
    ["How does meiosis differ from mitosis?", "Meiosis has two divisions and halves chromosome number, typically producing four genetically different haploid cells from one diploid cell. In animals it forms gametes and creates new combinations of inherited chromosomes."],
    ["What is a stem cell?", "An unspecialised cell that can divide by mitosis and produce daughter cells that become specialised. Bone marrow stem cells can generate different blood cells."],
    ["How do you construct a monohybrid cross?", "Define allele symbols, state parental genotypes, list gametes with one allele each, combine them in a Punnett square, then give offspring genotypes, phenotypes and probabilities."],
    ["What does a Tt × Tt cross predict when T is dominant?", "Genotypes: 1 TT : 2 Tt : 1 tt. Phenotypes: 3 dominant : 1 recessive. Each offspring has a 1/4 probability of tt; small families need not match the exact ratio."],
    ["How does a test cross reveal an unknown genotype?", "Cross the dominant-phenotype individual with a homozygous recessive individual. Any recessive offspring proves the unknown parent carries the recessive allele. All-dominant offspring in a small sample does not prove homozygosity."],
    ["What is codominance in ABO blood groups?", "Both Iᴬ and Iᴮ are expressed together, producing AB. Each dominates Iᴼ. Group A can be IᴬIᴬ or IᴬIᴼ; B can be IᴮIᴮ or IᴮIᴼ; O is IᴼIᴼ."],
    ["How does sex inheritance work in the course model?", "Females are XX and males XY. Eggs carry X; sperm carry X or Y. An XX × XY cross predicts equal probabilities of XX and XY offspring."],
    ["Why is an X-linked recessive condition more easily expressed in males?", "A male has only one X, so an affected allele there has no corresponding dominant allele on Y to mask it. A carrier mother and unaffected father have a 1/2 affected probability for each son, or 1/4 across all children under equal sex probabilities."]
  ],
  18: [
    ["How do continuous and discontinuous variation differ?", "Continuous variation has a range with intermediates, such as height, often influenced by many genes and environment. Discontinuous variation has distinct categories, such as ABO blood group. Use a histogram for grouped continuous data and a bar chart for categories."],
    ["How do mutation and recombination produce variation?", "Mutation changes genetic material and can create new alleles. Meiosis and random fertilisation create new combinations of existing alleles. Mutations arise randomly, rather than because an organism needs them."],
    ["What is an adaptive feature, and what does fitness mean?", "An adaptive feature is inherited and improves survival and reproduction in a particular environment. Fitness is the likelihood of surviving and reproducing in that environment."],
    ["What is the sequence of natural selection?", "Inherited variation exists. More offspring are produced than resources support. Selection pressures favour some variants, which survive and reproduce more. Their offspring inherit the alleles, which become more common over generations."],
    ["Why do populations evolve rather than individuals?", "Evolution is a change in inherited characteristics across generations. Selection changes the frequencies of alleles in a population; an individual does not purposefully acquire useful mutations to survive."],
    ["How are xerophytes and hydrophytes adapted?", "Xerophytes may have thick cuticles, reduced leaves, sunken stomata or hairs to reduce water loss. Hydrophytes may have large air spaces for buoyancy and gas movement, and upper-surface stomata on floating leaves."],
    ["How does selective breeding work?", "Humans choose parents with useful inherited features, breed them, select suitable offspring and repeat across generations. It differs from natural selection because humans choose who breeds. Inbreeding can reduce variation and expose harmful recessive alleles."],
    ["Why can a sickle-cell allele persist where malaria is common?", "People with one affected allele usually avoid sickle-cell disease and gain protection against severe malaria. Their survival and reproduction can maintain the allele, despite harmful effects in individuals with two affected alleles."]
  ],
  19: [
    ["How do population, community and ecosystem differ?", "A population is one species in the same area at the same time. A community is all the populations there. An ecosystem is the community interacting with its physical environment."],
    ["What do producers, consumers and decomposers do?", "Producers make organic nutrients, usually by photosynthesis. Consumers feed on other organisms. Decomposers obtain energy from dead or waste organic material, recycling elements."],
    ["What do food-chain arrows and trophic levels represent?", "Arrows point from food to feeder, showing energy transfer. A trophic level is a feeding position. Producers occupy the first level; a primary consumer eats producers."],
    ["Why is less energy available at higher trophic levels?", "Some material is uneaten or undigested. Respiration transfers energy to the environment as heat. Therefore less energy supports higher consumers; transfer efficiency is not always 10%."],
    ["How is energy-transfer efficiency calculated?", "Energy at the next trophic level ÷ energy at the previous level × 100. Compare values for the same area and time. For 2400 kJ from 20 000 kJ, efficiency is 12%."],
    ["Why can a pyramid of numbers be inverted?", "It counts organisms without accounting for their size. One large tree can support many insects, so a producer level may contain fewer individuals than the consumer level."],
    ["How do pyramids of biomass and energy differ?", "Biomass shows total dry mass at a moment in time and can be inverted with rapid producer turnover. Energy shows transfer per area per time and decreases at successive levels when measured consistently."],
    ["Which processes move carbon through the carbon cycle?", "Photosynthesis removes carbon dioxide into carbohydrates. Feeding transfers organic carbon. Plant, animal and decomposer respiration returns carbon dioxide. Combustion releases stored carbon; some buried material forms fossil fuels over geological time."],
    ["Is energy recycled in ecosystems?", "No. Energy flows mainly from sunlight through organisms and ultimately dissipates as heat. Elements such as carbon and nitrogen are recycled."],
    ["What is nitrogen fixation?", "Conversion of atmospheric nitrogen into usable nitrogen compounds by bacteria in soil or legume root nodules. Most plants cannot use nitrogen gas directly; roots absorb nitrate ions."],
    ["How does decomposition return nitrogen to soil?", "Bacteria and fungi break down nitrogen-containing material in dead organisms and waste, releasing ammonium ions. Plants use absorbed nitrogen to make amino acids and proteins."],
    ["How do nitrification and denitrification differ?", "Nitrification uses oxygen as bacteria convert ammonium to nitrite then nitrate. Denitrification occurs in oxygen-poor conditions and converts nitrate to nitrogen gas, reducing soil fertility."],
    ["What changes population size?", "Births and immigration increase it; deaths and emigration decrease it. Food, competition, disease and predation can limit growth. Carrying capacity is the population an environment can support sustainably under current conditions."],
    ["Why do lag and exponential phases occur in a microbial culture?", "During lag, cells adapt and make necessary molecules, so numbers rise slowly. During exponential growth, abundant resources allow frequent division and increasingly rapid population growth."],
    ["Why do stationary and death phases follow in a closed culture?", "Nutrients or oxygen become limiting and waste accumulates. In the stationary phase, divisions roughly balance deaths. In the death phase, conditions worsen and deaths exceed new cells formed."]
  ],
  20: [
    ["What is biodiversity, and what threatens it?", "The variety of species in an area. Habitat destruction, pollution, overharvesting, introduced species and climate change can reduce it and cause extinction."],
    ["How can farming increase food production?", "Fertilisers supply limiting mineral nutrients; pesticides and herbicides reduce crop damage or competition. Machinery improves cultivation efficiency. Selective breeding and genetic modification can provide useful crop and livestock traits."],
    ["What are disadvantages of monocultures and intensive livestock farming?", "Monocultures reduce habitat diversity and can favour rapid pest spread. Crowded livestock can spread infections and produce polluting waste. Unnecessary antibiotic use selects resistant bacteria."],
    ["How can deforestation cause erosion, flooding and higher carbon dioxide?", "Removing roots leaves soil unbound; rain and wind remove topsoil. Reduced interception and water uptake increase runoff. Fewer trees photosynthesise, while burning and decomposition release stored carbon."],
    ["What is the sequence of eutrophication?", "Nitrates/phosphates enter water → algal growth → light blocked → plants and algae die → decomposers multiply and respire → dissolved oxygen falls → aquatic animals may die. Fertiliser does not directly use up oxygen."],
    ["Why can raw sewage reduce dissolved oxygen?", "Its organic material feeds microorganisms. Their aerobic respiration during decomposition consumes dissolved oxygen. Treatment removes solids and reduces organic waste and pathogens."],
    ["How do greenhouse gases contribute to warming?", "Carbon dioxide and methane absorb and re-emit outgoing infrared radiation, reducing heat loss to space. Higher concentrations enhance the natural greenhouse effect, affecting climate, habitats and sea level."],
    ["Why are non-biodegradable plastics harmful?", "They persist in ecosystems. Animals may become entangled, or swallow plastic that blocks the gut and reduces feeding. Reuse, recycling and careful waste collection reduce these risks."],
    ["How can forests and fisheries be managed sustainably?", "Resources must be replenished at least as fast as they are used. Replanting and controlled logging protect forests. Fishing quotas, larger net mesh, reserves and closed breeding seasons allow fish to survive and reproduce."],
    ["How can conservation protect endangered species?", "Protect habitats, restrict hunting, use captive breeding and reintroduction, and preserve seeds in seed banks. Maintain genetic diversity. Assisted reproduction can help breeding, but released organisms still need a suitable protected habitat."]
  ],
  21: [
    ["How do biotechnology and genetic modification differ?", "Biotechnology uses organisms or biological processes to make useful products or provide services. Genetic modification directly changes genetic material by inserting, removing or altering genes."],
    ["Why are microorganisms useful in biotechnology?", "They reproduce rapidly under suitable conditions, grow in large quantities and can make useful complex molecules. Bacterial plasmids are useful gene-transfer vectors. Yeast and Penicillium are fungi, rather than bacteria."],
    ["How is yeast used in bread-making and biofuel production?", "Anaerobic respiration converts glucose to ethanol and carbon dioxide. Carbon dioxide makes dough rise; ethanol evaporates during baking. Ethanol can also be collected for biofuel."],
    ["How are protease, lipase, pectinase and lactase used?", "Protease removes protein stains; lipase removes grease. Pectinase breaks down pectin to improve fruit-juice yield. Lactase splits lactose into glucose and galactose for lactose-free milk."],
    ["Why must industrial fermenters be sterile and well mixed?", "Sterility reduces contamination and competing microbes. Stirring distributes nutrients, oxygen and heat, maintaining suitable conditions throughout the culture."],
    ["Why control temperature, pH and oxygen in a fermenter?", "Temperature and pH affect enzyme activity. Cooling removes heat released by metabolism. Sterile air supplies oxygen for aerobic processes such as penicillin production; industrial fermentation is not always anaerobic."],
    ["What do restriction enzymes, ligase and plasmids do?", "Restriction enzymes cut DNA at specific sequences. DNA ligase joins fragments. Plasmids carry inserted genes into cells as vectors; joined DNA from different sources is recombinant DNA."],
    ["What are the steps for producing human insulin using bacteria?", "Obtain insulin-coding DNA → cut it and a plasmid with the same restriction enzyme → join with ligase → introduce recombinant plasmids into bacteria → select and culture the modified bacteria → collect and purify insulin."],
    ["What useful traits can genetic modification give crops?", "Herbicide resistance lets crops survive a particular herbicide. Insect resistance reduces damage from certain pests. Golden Rice contains beta-carotene, which humans can convert to vitamin A."],
    ["What benefits and concerns should be considered for GM crops?", "Potential benefits include higher yield, improved nutrition and reduced insecticide use. Concerns include gene transfer to compatible wild relatives, resistant pests or weeds, effects on non-target organisms and seed costs. Outcomes depend on the trait and management."]
  ]
};
